<h3>In Code</h3>
<div class="code-wrap">
  <pre><code class="usage language-javascript">
    {`
  // import the component
  import "../lib/lambo";

  //use it in your HTML
  <rex-lambo src="./lambo-transparent.png" speed="0.2"></rex-lambo>

  //attributes
  //  src    - image url (default ./lambo-transparent.png)
  //  avatar - photo for the player profile billboard (default ./me.jpg)
  //  speed  - seconds per wheel revolution (default 0.2, smaller is faster)
  //  paused - freezes the wheels, road and billboards
  //  fill   - runs full screen in the box it is given (see the downloadable bundle's index.html)
  //
  //  The car sits parked until a key press or tap (not Tab, Shift, Escape, F keys or a held-down
  //  key), drives to a billboard, stops, and carries on to the next one at the following key press
  //  or tap. A swipe left does the same as a tap, and a swipe right drives round to the previous
  //  billboard, straight past the others. The mini billboards on the progress strip drive straight to
  //  that stop, TURBO doubles
  //  the speed, and the speaker switches the music on and off.
  `}
  </code></pre>
  <br />
  <section class="highlight">
    <h3>How it works</h3>
    Each wheel is a square box with a round rim inside it. The box is rotated and squashed with
    <code>rotate()</code> and <code>scaleX()</code> so the circle becomes the same tilted ellipse as the wheel in the picture.
    Inside, a spokes and hub layer turns with a CSS keyframe animation, so it spins within the squashed shape and keeps the
    perspective. The road is a long flat strip turned with <code>rotateZ()</code> and laid back with <code
      >rotateX()</code
    > inside a CSS <code>perspective</code>, so the browser foreshortens the grid. Only its background position
    animates, one road block per loop, so it never ends. A pixel art billboard is added on the far side of the road each
    time the car sets off, and the car brakes to a stop beside it. Each is a tiny 64 x 32 canvas painted in JS and
    scaled up with <code>image-rendering: pixelated</code>, stood upright inside the same tilted 3D space as the road so
    it gets the same perspective, and slid along at road speed. A retrowave highway barrier runs along the far edge of
    the road: a tall dark indigo wall with a neon magenta top, a glowing cyan band and lamps, with a two tube neon guard
    rail on top. Each part is a set of CSS gradients repeated along a long upright face and scrolled by sliding its
    background. The sky fades from black at the top to deep purple at the horizon, with faint stars twinkling on a
    canvas. A big retrowave moon hangs behind the city: a CSS gradient disc with horizontal slits cut through its lower
    half by a mask, and a soft halo. A wireframe skyline stands in the distance on the far side of the road: plain boxes
    whose faces are neon outlines in the same tilted 3D space, in a pattern that repeats so it can scroll with the road
    forever. Now and then a shooting star crosses the sky. Once the car has set off, music plays and an analyser reads
    its spectrum, so the windows of the skyline light up from the ground as a graphic equaliser (without music they flip
    on and off at random). To keep it light, nothing is drawn while the component is scrolled out of view or paused, and
    a frame is only redrawn when something has moved.
  </section>
</div>

